Description: vmwgfx: fix CPU map offset, surface flags, format and CPU/GPU sync
 Five defects in the vmwgfx backend, found while chasing ARC software-decoded
 video that first composited black and then played its opening frames on a loop.
 .
 1. get_map_handle() recombined the halves stashed by store_map_handle() with
 '&' instead of '|'. That is 0 for every possible input: the shifted high half
 has no low bits and blob_id is a uint32_t with no high bits. Since
 store_map_handle() also sets memory_idx = 0xFF at create time,
 exist_map_handle() is always true, so every CPU map of a locally created bo
 mmap'd offset 0 rather than buffer_map_handle - every frame a software decoder
 wrote landed outside the buffer. vmwgfx_bo_map() never checked MAP_FAILED and
 logged "map success" unconditionally, so nothing ever showed it.
 .
 2. SVGA3D_SURFACE_SCREENTARGET was requested for every format. A screen target
 has to be something the host can put on a screen - a displayable RGB format -
 so asking for it on the YUV/R8 video formats made an illegal surface and the
 host refused to DEFINE it. The distinction was already known here (is_texture()
 gates drm_vmw_surface_flag_scanout) and simply was not applied to svga3d_flags;
 texture formats now ask for HINT_TEXTURE | BIND_SHADER_RESOURCE instead.
 .
 3. The planar YUV formats claimed packed video formats - SVGA3D_UYVY for YV12,
 SVGA3D_NV12 for NV12. What is actually allocated is a single 8-bit byte plane:
 vmwgfx_surface_extent() stacks the chroma planes under the luma plane at height
 3*ceil(h/2) and the stride is one byte per pixel. UYVY is two bytes per pixel,
 so the host also computed a MOB twice the size of the one allocated.
 SVGA3D_R8_UNORM describes the real layout. Consumers are unaffected: they
 import the DMA-BUF and read the layout from the DRM fourcc and the per-plane
 strides/offsets in the handle.
 .
 Together (2) and (3) were worth 966 rejected commands per 10s of playback,
 measured on device with drm.debug=0x2 - 17 DEFINE_GB_SURFACE, 17
 BIND_GB_SURFACE, and then everything naming those sids afterwards, including
 300 DX_UPDATE_SUBRESOURCE issued by Mesa itself. All of it fails
 asynchronously: the ioctl returns success and the device fails the command
 buffer later, so user space never sees an error. And each rejection lands in
 vmw_cmdbuf_work_func(), which blocks submission on every SVGA command-buffer
 context, preempts them, re-queues the outstanding buffers and restarts the
 contexts - two host round-trips stalling the entire guest's graphics command
 stream, ~65 times a second during 30fps playback. That was the stutter. It is
 now 0 rejections.
 .
 4. The backend supplied neither .bo_invalidate nor .bo_flush, and drv.c
 silently no-ops when those hooks are absent (drv.c:629/644) - no CPU/GPU
 synchronisation for mapped buffers at all. Implemented with DRM_VMW_SYNCCPU
 grab/release, resolving the MOB handle the ioctl wants via
 DRM_VMW_GB_SURFACE_REF (crep.buffer_handle), plus UPDATE_GB_SURFACE from
 bo_flush to publish CPU writes to the host.
 .
 bo_invalidate deliberately does NOT issue a readback. READBACK_GB_SURFACE
 copies host -> MOB, which is the wrong direction for these buffers: the guest
 MOB is the authoritative copy, since they are written by the CPU and only read
 by the GPU. On a read lock it overwrites the frame the decoder just wrote with
 whatever the host last held.
 .
 5. drm_vmw_surface_flag_coherent was not requested. Without it the host reads
 the guest pages once, when it first realises the resource, and never looks
 again. COHERENT makes the kernel write-protect the backing pages, record dirty
 ranges as they fault and flush them on validation - what the kernel's own
 dumb-buffer path asks for in exactly this situation (vmwgfx_surface.c:
 scanout|shareable|create_buffer|coherent). Note it only covers writes through
 the vmwgfx GEM mapping in the creating process; a prime/DMA-BUF mapping in
 another process still needs the explicit flush in (4).
 .
 None of these five was the cause of the frame loop itself - that was
 SurfaceFlinger running AOSP-13's default Skia RenderEngine, which has no
 fallback for a driver that cannot import YUV dmabufs and caches one host-side
 texture snapshot per buffer. See fydroid_rt_apple.mk. They are all real, and
 the stutter and the 966 command-buffer stalls were genuinely theirs.
Author: FydeOS <yang@fydeos.io>

--- a/vmwgfx.c
+++ b/vmwgfx.c
@@ -89,7 +89,17 @@
 
 static uint64_t get_map_handle(struct bo_metadata *meta) {
   uint64_t map_handle = meta->map_info;
-  return (map_handle << 32) & meta->blob_id;
+  /*
+   * Recombine the halves stashed by store_map_handle(). This was '&', which
+   * is 0 for every possible input: the shifted high half has no low bits and
+   * blob_id is a uint32_t with no high bits. Every CPU map therefore mmap'd
+   * offset 0 instead of buffer_map_handle, so everything the CPU wrote - i.e.
+   * every frame out of a software video decoder - landed outside the SVGA
+   * surface. The GPU then sampled an untouched surface and composited the
+   * video as black, while the decoder, SurfaceFlinger and Chrome all reported
+   * perfectly healthy frames.
+   */
+  return (map_handle << 32) | meta->blob_id;
 }
 
